Every Aspect of this game is stellar. The aesthetic of this game is so meticulously crafted and pristine, the core mechanics are so perfectly intertwined in every aspect, the progression path and the map with the mobility mechanics, the mobs and bosses of every region progressively get more complex as mechanics pile up and the feeling of difficulty never feel unfair. Any time you feel as though the game is unbalanced you recognize their is a mechanic you aren't utilizing properly and the room for improvement and still open for many approaches to conquering a challenge (no one charm at any point in the game is the best, play styles are all quintessential and marginal, so long as the player is comfortable and capable
). All while simultaneously presenting the player room for self-psychoanalysis via reflection on how they interpreted the grim depth of the nature of the game intrinsically. Lots of this game reflects of the prospect of mankind and the human condition through "bugkind" and the infection. However the most incredible feat of this aspect of the games storytelling is that the Vessel you control is merely that, Hollow, devoid of emotions and expression, and will never mutter a single line of dialogue. This allows for the lore of Hollow Knight to be presented by the things embodied within Hallownest and the reflection upon how the player views the Vessel’s position in its cyclical nature separate from each other, leaving this reflection unbiased from the developers and immaculately eye opening for the player.
I also personally loved the aspect of breaking the “mask” or facade that everyone develops and peering into the real aspirations of every encounterable npc, enemy, and even certain objects. Understanding what it is that others dream about, then taking that one step further in 1.2 (Grimme Troupe
) and the prospect of unsubtle Nightmares. Gathering the beliefs and yearnings from the depths of this twisted kingdom yet remaining unfalteringly moor.
However that isn't to say their doesn't exist lore within Hallownest itself, if that suits your taste. The lore aspect is also very will combined with the world around you and as you progress every nook and cranny presents opportunity for discovery and truly is the embodiment of a metroidvania.
I have no idea where Team Cherry could begin to improve on this game. To give a small example of the degree to which this game takes its depth, I want to do a brief overview of the Mantis Lords boss fight.

